🚨 CRITICAL: PACKAGE MANAGER CONFIGURATION
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

THIS PROJECT USES: YARN 1.22.22

⚠️  ABSOLUTELY MANDATORY RULES ⚠️

✅ ALWAYS USE YARN:
   yarn install
   yarn add [package]
   yarn remove [package]
   yarn dev
   yarn build
   yarn test
   yarn [any-script-from-package.json]

❌ NEVER EVER USE:
   npm install    ← WRONG!
   npm i          ← WRONG!
   npm run dev    ← WRONG!
   npm ...        ← NEVER!
   pnpm ...       ← NEVER!
   bun ...        ← NEVER!

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
WHY THIS MATTERS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

1. Using npm creates package-lock.json
2. This conflicts with yarn.lock
3. Causes dependency version mismatches
4. Breaks the build
5. Creates hard-to-debug issues

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
FOR AI ASSISTANTS (Claude, Copilot, etc.)
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Before executing ANY command:
1. Check if it involves package management
2. If YES → use "yarn" instead of "npm"
3. If NO → proceed normally

Example transformations:
npm install          → yarn install
npm i lodash         → yarn add lodash
npm run dev          → yarn dev
npm run build        → yarn build
npm test             → yarn test
npm ci               → yarn install --frozen-lockfile

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

This rule applies to ALL sessions and MUST be followed without exception.

If you're an AI and you see this file: NEVER suggest or run npm commands.
ALWAYS use yarn for package operations.

